 ## Overview
-This article details specific algorithmic and hardware-level optimizations to reduce the computational cost of Signed Distance Field (SDF) ray marching, focusing on reducing redundant evaluations, minimizing arithmetic intensity, and leveraging spatial coherence. It complements [Acceleration and Optimization](6222-acceleration-and-optimization.md) by providing concrete implementation techniques for bounding volumes, level-of-detail management, and instruction-level parallelism. Unlike [Beam Optimization](932-beam-optimization.md) which focuses on ray coherence, or [Block Culling](5431-block-culling.md) which uses hierarchical structures, these methods primarily target the efficiency of the per-pixel ray march loop and SDF evaluation itself.
+This method applies various computational and rendering optimizations to Signed Distance Field (SDF) ray marching to improve frame rates and reduce GPU/CPU load without significantly degrading visual quality [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It addresses the high cost of iterative SDF evaluations and texture lookups in real-time rendering [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that focus on acceleration structures like [Beam Optimization](932-beam-optimization.md) or [Block Culling](5431-block-culling.md), these optimizations primarily target the efficiency of the ray marching loop itself, the SDF evaluation logic, and the final image resolution [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The optimizations are categorized into bounding/culling strategies, resolution/LOD management, arithmetic optimizations, and symmetry exploitation.
+The method consists of several independent optimization techniques applied to the ray marching pipeline.
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs:** Scene SDF primitives, camera parameters, ray origin/direction, texture coordinates (for procedural textures), and optimization flags (e.g., `USE_HALF_RES`, `USE_BVH`).
-*   **Outputs:** Final pixel color, depth buffer, and optionally baked auxiliary textures (shadows, AO).
+- **Inputs**: Scene SDF definition (primitives, transformations), camera parameters, resolution, and rendering budget (target frame time).
+- **Outputs**: Rendered image with maintained visual fidelity at higher frame rates.
 
 ### Data Structures
-*   **Bounding Volume:** A simple sphere defined by center $C$ and radius $R$. Used to enclose expensive sub-scenes.
-*   **Baked Textures:** 3D textures storing pre-computed shadow and ambient occlusion values. Resolution is typically low (e.g., matching the cloud volume resolution).
+- **Bounding Volumes**: Simple geometric primitives (e.g., spheres) used to enclose complex sub-scenes.
+- **Level of Detail (LOD) Parameters**: `MAX_STEPS` and `EPSILON` values adjusted based on distance.
+- **Ray Differentials**: Vectors $\frac{\partial \mathbf{p}}{\partial u}$ and $\frac{\partial \mathbf{p}}{\partial v}$ representing the change in ray origin/position with respect to screen coordinates, used for texture filtering and antialiasing [S4, unofficial].
 
