 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Varies by optimization. Bounding volume early-outs reduce average case complexity by skipping sub-scenes. LOD reduces steps for distant objects. MMX optimization reduces instruction count for interpolation. Symmetry reduces primitive evaluations by a factor related to the symmetry order.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Baked textures (shadow/AO) increase memory usage proportional to the volume resolution. Half-resolution rendering reduces memory bandwidth usage for color/depth buffers.
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a 300-MHz Pentium-II, replacing floating-point with integer arithmetic yielded 4-6x speedup. MMX provided 0% improvement on Pentium-II but 60-80% on Pentium. Hand-coded address generation improved performance by up to 15% [S4].
-*   **Strengths:** Significant FPS gains in real-time applications. Effective for static scenes (baking) and symmetric objects.
-*   **Limitations:** Over-relaxation causes artifacts. Baking is limited to static scenes. MMX is obsolete on modern GPUs. Half-resolution can introduce aliasing if not upscaled properly.
+- **Time Complexity**: Varies by optimization. Standard sphere tracing is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of steps. Bounding volume early-outs and LOD reduce the average $N$ for distant or complex scenes. MMX interpolation reduces instruction count per voxel sample [S3]. Wave surfing is $O(n)$ for height maps, potentially $O(\log n)$ with quadtree acceleration [S5, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: Minimal additional memory for bounding volumes and ray differentials. SDF texture baking requires storing a 3D texture [S1, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance**: On a 300-MHz Pentium-II, replacing floating-point with integer arithmetic provided 4-6x speedup, while MMX provided 0% improvement on Pentium-II but 60-80% on Pentium [S3]. Hand-coded address generation improved performance by up to 15% [S3].
+- **Strengths**: Significant frame rate improvements in real-time rendering, especially at high resolutions or with complex scenes. Simple to implement for bounding volumes and LOD.
+- **Limitations**: Over-relaxation can cause visual artifacts. MMX optimization is hardware-specific and obsolete. Wave surfing performs poorly on flat landscapes.
+- **When to Use**: Real-time rendering where frame rate is critical. Use bounding volumes for complex sub-scenes. Use LOD for distant objects. Use resolution scaling for high-resolution displays.
+- **When Not to Use**: When visual precision is paramount (avoid over-relaxation). When targeting modern GPUs where MMX is irrelevant. When the scene is uniformly complex with no distant parts (LOD benefits are minimal).
 
 ## Background
-The techniques described originate from various sources in real-time graphics, including Shadertoy examples (2014-2019) by artists like Inigo Quilez and others, blog posts on volumetric cloud rendering, and academic papers on I/O-conscious volume rendering for older hardware (Pentium era) [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4][S5, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. The core principles of bounding volume early-outs and LOD are widely adopted in modern SDF renderers to maintain real-time frame rates.
+The techniques described originate from various sources in the real-time rendering community, including Shadertoy examples and academic papers on volume rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3][S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. Sphere tracing was introduced by John C. Hart in 1996 [S2, unofficial]. The specific optimizations like bounding volume early-outs, LOD, and over-relaxation are commonly cited in online tutorials and unofficial guides [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. MMX optimizations were explored in the early 2000s for CPU-based volume rendering [S3]. Ray differentials for antialiasing and filtering were demonstrated in Shadertoy examples from 2014-2015 [S4, unofficial]. Wave surfing optimizations were discussed in the context of voxel landscape engines in the mid-2000s [S5, unofficial].
 
